{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic"],"declarations":28,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.interface_never_equals_isospin_weight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.interface_never_equals_isospin_weight","kind":"theorem","line":704,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L704-L720","doc":"**Therefore no admissible interface element equals an isospin weight.**\n\nThe blocker in its sharpest form. `qou-17gn` posed the colour-weight blocker as\n\"`span{1, σ₂}` reaches only a 2-plane of the 4-dimensional `V_(2,1)` block\".\nThis is the same obstruction read off on the observable side, where it is\nquantitative: the two targets miss the reachable interval by 63 % and 23 %.\n\nA repair must therefore change the *arena*, not the parameter — option C5,\nenlarging `H₃` to `H₄`, or a genuinely different pairing between the isospin\nlabel and the bond. Tuning `d`, choosing a crossing sign, or picking a different\n`q` cannot close a gap the band is on the wrong side of. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:cor:interface-never-realises-isospin-weight","label":"cor:interface-never-realises-isospin-weight","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/interface-never-realises-isospin-weight.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.band_strictly_between","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.band_strictly_between","kind":"theorem","line":692,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L692-L702","doc":"**The whole admissible band sits strictly between the two isospin weights.**\n\nEvery `μ = z + d` with `d ∈ [−h, 0]` satisfies `μ₋ < μ < μ₊`. Combining\n`mu_mem_Icc` (the band) with the two separations above. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:interface-band-between-isospin-weights","label":"prop:interface-band-between-isospin-weights","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/interface-band-between-isospin-weights.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_below_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_below_floor","kind":"theorem","line":681,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L681-L690","doc":"**The T = 0 weight is below the interface floor.**\n\n`μ₋ < z − h`. The hypothesis is the reduced inequality\n`q − z < (z − h)(q + q⁻¹)`; at `q₀` under D the sides are `0.497291` and\n`0.811654`, and the boundary is at `q = 1.465571`. Under C the inequality\nFAILS — `μ₋` is inside the band there — so this theorem is stated as a\nconditional and its hypothesis must be checked per convention. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_above_ceiling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_above_ceiling","kind":"theorem","line":672,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L672-L679","doc":"**The T = 1 weight is above the interface ceiling.**\n\n`μ₊ > z`. The hypothesis is the reduced inequality `z(q + q⁻¹) < z + q⁻¹`, which\nfor `z = q³/(q²+1)` is equivalent to `q⁵ < q² + 1` and so holds for\n`q < 1.193859…`. At `q₀` the two sides are `1.68490` and `2.23205`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_eq_zero_of_conventionD'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_eq_zero_of_conventionD'","kind":"theorem","line":609,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L609-L624","doc":"Over an ordered field the denominator hypothesis discharges itself: both\nfactors are sums of even powers and `1`, hence strictly positive at every `q`.\n\nOrdering is the weakest thing that decides it, so this stays generic rather\nthan specialising to `ℝ` — §7c, and it keeps this file free of\n`Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic`. (`LinearOrderedField` is deprecated in this Mathlib;\nthe three-class spelling is its replacement.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_eq_zero_of_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_eq_zero_of_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":570,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L570-L601","doc":"**Convention D is exactly the trace that annihilates the sign block.**\n\n`z_D = q³/(q²+1)`, written here without division as `z·(q²+1) = q³`, forces\n`y₍₁³₎ = 0`. So the radical is not an accident of convention D that has to be\nworked around — it *is* convention D. The other root of the closed form,\n`z = q`, is the trivial trace and is excluded separately.\n\nThis is what every `*_of_radical` lemma above was assuming.\n\n**Relation to `RowCapObstruction.lean`.** That file proves the same vanishing\nfrom the representation side: `weyl_dim_alt_eq_zero_iff`, `weylDimAlt N = 0 ↔\nN < 3` — below three rows the alternating block does not exist, so nothing can\nweight it. This lemma comes at it from the trace side: among all Markov\nparameters `z`, the ones killing that block are exactly `z = q` and `z = z_D`.\n\nNeither subsumes the other, and the pair is the content: `z_D` is not merely\n*compatible with* the `N = 2` cap, it is the unique non-trivial trace parameter\nthat implements it. The header above already asserted this (\"the degeneracy is\nlikewise structural: it *is* the `N = 2` row cap ... seen through the Gram\nform\"); until now that was an observation relating two facts, with no lemma\nconnecting them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_closed_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.antisym_weight_closed_form","kind":"theorem","line":528,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L528-L568","doc":"**The antisymmetric weight in closed form — the premise, not a hypothesis.**\n\nEvery `*_of_radical` result above takes `y₍₁³₎ = 0` as a HYPOTHESIS. That is\nhonest as far as it goes, but nothing in this file established *why* the\nweight vanishes, so the conditional results rested on an unproved premise —\nthe pattern AGENTS.md names \"conditional-proved ≠ premise-established\". This\nlemma supplies it.\n\nSolving `tr_M = Σ_λ y_λ χ_λ` on `H₃(q)` at the identity, at `σ₀`, and at\n`e⁻ = σ₀σ₁σ₀` determines all three weights. The `e⁻` row is where the\ncorrected `tr_M(e⁻) = h·z² + z` enters (it was `z³` before 2026-05-19), so\nthis identity is a statement about the *corrected* trace specifically.\n\nThe three character values used, from the `H₃(q)` table:\n\n| | `1` | `σ₀` | `e⁻` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `χ₍₃₎` | `1` | `q` | `q³` |\n| `χ₍₂,₁₎` | `2` | `q − q⁻¹` | `0` |\n| `χ₍₁³₎` | `1` | `−q⁻¹` | `−q⁻³` |\n\nStated multiplied through by the denominator, so the conclusion itself needs no\nnon-vanishing hypothesis and no division.\n\n`q² ≠ 1` IS required, and is not a technicality: `χ₍₂,₁₎(σ₀) = q − q⁻¹`\nvanishes there, so at `q² = 1` the `σ₀` row carries no information about\n`y₍₂,₁₎` and the three rows stop determining the weights. That is the\nclassical `S₃` degeneration — `H₃(1) = ℂ[S₃]`, where the Markov parameter no\nlonger separates the blocks. The substrate sits at `q₀ ≈ 1.11`, comfortably\naway from it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_eq_one_sub_y21_of_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_eq_one_sub_y21_of_radical","kind":"theorem","line":511,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L511-L520","doc":"The triplet counterpart, for completeness: with `y₍₁³₎ = 0`,\n`μ₊ = y₍₃₎ + y₍₂,₁₎ = 1 − y₍₂,₁₎`, i.e. `0.75270211325196540159` at `q₀`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_eq_y21_of_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_eq_y21_of_radical","kind":"theorem","line":488,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L488-L509","doc":"**THE PUNCHLINE. When the `(1³)` block is the radical, the isospin singlet\ndoes not notice.**\n\nSet `y₍₁³₎ = 0` — convention D, where `y₍₁³₎` vanishes identically, not merely\nnumerically. Then `μ₋ = y₍₂,₁₎` exactly: the T = 0 channel is carried entirely\nby the mixed-symmetry block. Quotienting `H₃(q)` by its radical therefore\ndeletes a summand that was already contributing zero to the binding energy.\n\nAt `q₀`, both sides are `0.24729788674803459840622064838` — and the same number\nis `1/[2]_q²`, the recorded closed form for `y₍₂,₁₎` under D, and the recorded\nvalue of `μ₋ = μ_pp · μ_nn`. Three descriptions, one quantity, now with the\nreason rather than the coincidence.\n\nThis is the derivation that unblocks the Connes construction: the obstruction in\n`lip_kernel_forces_unbounded` is real, the quotient that removes it is free. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:branching-identity-isospin-weights","label":"prop:branching-identity-isospin-weights","md":"quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/branching-identity-isospin-weights.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_sum_eq_one_trivially","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_sum_eq_one_trivially","kind":"theorem","line":480,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L480-L486","doc":"The `μ`-side statement, for the record and to show it is *not* where the\ncontent lives: it needs neither `hone` nor `hz`, only that `q + q⁻¹ ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.branch_sum_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.branch_sum_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":462,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L462-L478","doc":"**`μ₊ + μ₋ = 1` is the normalisation seen through the branching.**\n\nStated on the branch sums rather than on `μ` directly, because on `μ` it is\nvacuous: `(z + q⁻¹) + (q − z) = q + q⁻¹` cancels `z` and holds with no\nhypotheses at all. The content is on the other side of the two branching\nidentities —\n\n    (y₍₃₎ + y₍₂,₁₎) + (y₍₂,₁₎ + y₍₁³₎) = y₍₃₎ + 2y₍₂,₁₎ + y₍₁³₎ = tr_M(1) = 1,\n\nwhere `y₍₂,₁₎` appears twice for the plainest possible reason: `dim (2,1) = 2`,\nso the mixed-symmetry block branches to *both* `H₂` blocks and is counted once\non each side. So `μ₊ + μ₋ = 1` is not an extra fact about isospin; it is\n`tr_M(1) = 1` restated, and any Markov trace whatever satisfies it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_eq_branch","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_singlet_eq_branch","kind":"theorem","line":447,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L447-L460","doc":"**The isospin singlet weight is the complementary sum over the branching.**\n\n`μ₋ · (q + q⁻¹) = q − z = (q + q⁻¹)(y₍₂,₁₎ + y₍₁³₎)`. Now it is `y₍₃₎` that\ncancels — `(3) ↓ (2)`, the totally symmetric block restricts entirely to the\nsymmetric block of `H₂` and cannot reach T = 0.\n\nTogether with the previous theorem this reproduces the whole `H₂ ⊂ H₃` Bratteli\ndiagram from two normalisations, and it makes `μ₊ + μ₋ = 1` a corollary of\n`tr_M(1) = 1` rather than an independent fact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_eq_branch","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.mu_triplet_eq_branch","kind":"theorem","line":430,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L430-L445","doc":"**The isospin triplet weight is a sum over the Bratteli branching.**\n\n`μ₊ · (q + q⁻¹) = z + q⁻¹ = (q + q⁻¹)(y₍₃₎ + y₍₂,₁₎)`, from the two\nnormalisations alone. The `(1³)` weight cancels identically — its character\nvalue `−q⁻¹` at `σ` is exactly offset by its multiplicity `1` at the identity,\nwhich is the branching statement `(1³) ↓ (1²)`: the totally antisymmetric block\nof `H₃` restricts entirely to the *antisymmetric* block of `H₂`, so it cannot\ncontribute to the symmetric (T = 1) weight.\n\nNo hypothesis on `q` beyond the field axioms — `q⁻¹` enters only linearly, so\n`ring` never needs `q ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.zero_weight_block_erasable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.zero_weight_block_erasable","kind":"theorem","line":413,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L413-L422","doc":"**A zero-weight block can be deleted from the trace.**\n\n`tr_M = Σ_λ y_λ χ_λ`. If `y l = 0` the `l` term is `0 · χ l = 0`, so the sum over\n`s.erase l` equals the sum over `s`. Trivial as arithmetic; load-bearing as\nphysics, because it is the statement that quotienting by the radical is\n*observationally inert* — no trace value moves, so no predicted number moves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.dirac_coefficients_convex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.dirac_coefficients_convex","kind":"theorem","line":345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L345-L355","doc":"**…and the two Dirac coefficients are a convex combination.**\n\n`q⁻¹ + (1 − q⁻¹) = 1`. At `q₀` that is `90.09 %` gradient and `9.908 %` Reeb.\nThe limits are `q = 1 ↦` pure gradient and `q → ∞ ↦` pure contact.\n\nCONSEQUENCE for `qou-5uj7`: because the operator MIXES the two directions, the\nmetric it induces is *not* a pure Reeb step count at finite `q` — the gradient\npart carries nine times the weight. The target theorem\n\"`d_Connes` = minimal Reeb step count\" can hold only in the `q → ∞` limit. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:connes-distance-not-reeb-step-count","label":"rem:connes-distance-not-reeb-step-count","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/connes-distance-not-reeb-step-count.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.reeb_coefficient_eq_hbar_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.reeb_coefficient_eq_hbar_q","kind":"theorem","line":329,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L329-L343","doc":"**The Reeb coefficient of the categorical Dirac operator is `ℏ_q`.**\n\n`D_∇ = q⁻¹∇ + (1 − q⁻¹)ι_ξ∇`, and the corpus's quantum Planck constant is\n`ℏ_q = (q − 1)/q`. These are the same number, identically:\n\n    1 − q⁻¹ = (q − 1)/q.\n\nMeasured agreement at `q₀`: `4.9e−32` at 30 dps, i.e. round-off. So `ℏ_q` is\nstructurally the Reeb-direction *weight* in the Dirac operator — a step cost —\nrather than an independent length. Bears on `qou-adwi`: it supports reading the\ntwo competing \"Planck lengths\" as the Reeb *share* (`ℏ_q ≈ 0.0991`) and the\nnormalising *total* (`ℓ_P ≡ 1`), rather than as contradictory values. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:reeb-coefficient-is-hbar-q","label":"prop:reeb-coefficient-is-hbar-q","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/reeb-coefficient-is-hbar-q.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.finite_distance_forces_trivial_kernel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.finite_distance_forces_trivial_kernel","kind":"theorem","line":304,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L304-L321","doc":"**…so a degenerate Gram form blocks the distance construction.**\n\nContrapositive packaging of the above: if the Connes distance IS finite —\ni.e. some `M` bounds the objective over the admissible set — then no kernel\nelement separates the two states. Under convention D the Markov–Gram form has a\nnontrivial radical, so the construction must first quotient by it. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:cor:finite-connes-forces-trivial-kernel","label":"cor:finite-connes-forces-trivial-kernel","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/finite-connes-forces-trivial-kernel.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.lip_kernel_forces_unbounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.lip_kernel_forces_unbounded","kind":"theorem","line":274,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L274-L302","doc":"**A seminorm with a nonzero kernel gives an unbounded Connes sup.**\n\nTHE LOAD-BEARING RESULT. Let `L` be the Lipschitz seminorm and `φ`, `ψ` two\nstates. The Connes distance is `sup {|φ a − ψ a| : L a ≤ 1}`. Suppose some `k`\nhas `L k = 0` while `φ k − ψ k ≠ 0` — exactly what a *degenerate* Gram form\nsupplies, its radical being the kernel. Then for every scalar `t` the element\n`a + t·k` still satisfies the constraint (`L` is absolutely homogeneous and\nsubadditive, so `L (a + t·k) ≤ L a`), while the objective grows linearly in `t`.\nSo no finite bound exists: for any proposed bound `M` there is an admissible\nelement exceeding it.\n\nStated as \"no upper bound\" rather than \"= ∞\" to stay inside an ordered field\nwithout adjoining a top element. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:gram-degeneracy-blocks-connes","label":"prop:gram-degeneracy-blocks-connes","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/gram-degeneracy-blocks-connes.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.markov_star_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.markov_star_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L254-L266","doc":"**Positivity of the whole Markov–Gram star form reduces to `y_λ ≥ 0`.**\n\n`tr_M(a a*) = Σ_λ y_λ · tr(A_λ A_λ*)`, and each `tr(A A*)` is a sum of squares\nby `starform_M2_sum_of_squares`. So a nonnegative combination of nonnegative\nterms is nonnegative: the star form is positive semidefinite **exactly when all\nMarkov weights are nonnegative**, and degenerate precisely on the blocks where\n`y_λ = 0`. Under convention D that is the single block `(1³)` — the row cap.\n\nStated for two blocks, which is the shape that matters here (`H₃` has the\n`y_λ > 0` blocks `(3)`, `(2,1)` and the `y = 0` block `(1³)`). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:markov-gram-star-positive","label":"prop:markov-gram-star-positive","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/markov-gram-star-positive.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_pos_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_pos_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L248-L252","doc":"**Definiteness: the star form vanishes only on zero.** Contrapositive of the\nsum-of-squares shape — a single nonzero entry already forces it positive. This\nis what upgrades the quotient-by-radical form from semidefinite to definite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L244-L246","doc":"**…and it is nonnegative.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_sum_of_squares","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.starform_M2_sum_of_squares","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L231-L242","doc":"**The star form is a sum of squares on each `M_d` block — hence positive.**\n\nWhere the bilinear `tr(AB)` splits `M₂` as `(3,1)`, the sesquilinear\n`tr(AA*)` is `Σ|a_ij|²`. On the real `M₂` parametrisation `A = [[a,b],[c,d]]`,\n\n    tr(A Aᵀ) = a² + b² + c² + d²,\n\nmanifestly nonnegative, and zero only when `A = 0`. This is why the Markov–Gram\nform flips from signature `(4,1)` to `(5,0)` the moment the correct Hecke\n`*`-structure `T_w* = T_{w⁻¹}` is used instead of plain multiplication. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L216-L222","doc":"**Symmetric and antisymmetric parts are orthogonal under the trace form.**\n\n`tr(SK) = 0` whenever `S` is symmetric and `K` antisymmetric, so the signature\nsplits cleanly as `(3,1)` with no cross terms. Shown here on the `M₂`\nparametrisation: the mixed terms cancel identically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_antisym_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_antisym_neg","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L207-L213","doc":"**…and the antisymmetric direction is negative.** Setting `α = δ = β = 0`\nleaves `−2γ²`, strictly negative unless `γ = 0`. This single direction is the\ntimelike one: `E₁₂ − E₂₁`, the rotation generator of the standard rep. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_sym_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2_sym_pos","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L200-L205","doc":"**…so the symmetric directions are positive.** Setting `γ = 0` leaves a sum\nof squares, strictly positive unless all vanish. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.traceform_M2","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L185-L198","doc":"**The trace form on `M₂` has signature `(3,1)`.**\n\nParametrise `A ∈ M₂` by its symmetric part `(α, δ, β)` and antisymmetric part\n`γ`, i.e. `A = [[α, β+γ], [β−γ, δ]]`. Then\n\n    tr(A²) = α² + 2β² − 2γ² + δ².\n\nThree squares enter with `+` and one with `−`: symmetric directions are\npositive, the single antisymmetric direction is negative. This is the whole\nreason the Markov–Gram form is Lorentzian — the `(2,1)` block of `H₃(q)` is an\n`M₂`, and `M₂` has exactly one antisymmetric direction. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:markov-gram-bilinear-vs-star","label":"rem:markov-gram-bilinear-vs-star","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/markov-gram-bilinear-vs-star.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.indefinite_of_pos_and_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.indefinite_of_pos_and_neg","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L165-L176","doc":"**Lorentzian signature: one negative direction alongside a positive one.**\n\nRecorded as the shape of the measurement — a form admitting both a strictly\npositive and a strictly negative value on the diagonal takes the value of\nneither sign uniformly, so it is indefinite. Under C the split is `(5,1)`;\nunder D, `(4,1)` plus a null direction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.neg_diag_not_posdef","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.GramSignature.neg_diag_not_posdef","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/GramSignature.lean#L152-L162","doc":"**A form with a negative diagonal entry is not positive semidefinite.**\n\nThe Markov–Gram matrix has a negative eigenvalue under both trace conventions,\nso in the eigenbasis some diagonal entry is negative. This is the elementary\nstatement that such a form cannot be an inner product — and therefore cannot be\nthe Riemannian metric a Connes distance would need. ","signatureChunk":0}]}